Yes — that would fit your use case much better than a traditional textbook.

What you’re describing is more like an **interview cram manual**: very short sections, pattern recognition, complexity reminders, tiny code templates, and “when to use this” cues. Something you can skim in 20–40 minutes before a test and use to reactivate knowledge you already learned.

I’d structure it like this:
- **1-page Big-O cheat sheet**
- **Arrays & strings:** common operations, traps, mini patterns
- **Hash maps & sets:** lookup/storage mental model + templates
- **Two pointers / sliding window:** trigger words and skeletons
- **Stack / queue:** when each appears in problems
- **Linked lists:** pointer patterns
- **Binary search:** exact template + boundary mistakes
- **Trees / graphs:** DFS/BFS templates
- **Heap:** top-k / priority problems
- **Sorting + intervals:** common interview patterns
- **Recursion/backtracking:** decision-tree template
- **Final “problem wording → likely pattern” page**

Each topic should probably have only four things: **recognize it, complexity, template, one tiny example**. No long theory unless it prevents a common mistake.

We can build that alongside your refresher, so every topic we review becomes a compact page in your personal DSA interview book. Then by the end, you’ll have both the learning path and the pre-interview skim guide.